From: Tiberiu Chibici <chibicitiberiu@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 04:03:48 +0200
Subject: byte_order: don't byte-reverse std::string objects

EndianReverse() reverses the bytes of whatever object it is given, and
toCommonEndian()/fromCommonEndian() call it on big-endian machines. The
CRC cache (platform_common.cpp) passes std::string values through them
(the game and git versions, the file path), so on big-endian (the PS3)
the strings' own pointer and length bytes were reversed: a SIGSEGV in a
SimpleTaskThread about 50 s after the start, at the main menu. A string is
a run of bytes, the same in either byte order: EndianReverse now returns
it unchanged.

Forwarded: not-needed (worth offering upstream)

diff --git a/source/shared_lib/include/platform/common/byte_order.h b/source/shared_lib/include/platform/common/byte_order.h
--- a/source/shared_lib/include/platform/common/byte_order.h
+++ b/source/shared_lib/include/platform/common/byte_order.h
@@ -13,6 +13,7 @@
 #define BYTE_ORDER_H
 
 #include <algorithm>
+#include <string>
 #include "leak_dumper.h"
 
 namespace Shared{ namespace PlatformByteOrder {
@@ -32,6 +33,13 @@ template<class T> T EndianReverse(T t) {
     return t;
 }
 
+// A string is a run of bytes, the same in either byte order; reversing its object's own bytes (its
+// pointer and length) corrupted it on big-endian machines: the CRC cache (platform_common.cpp) wrote
+// and read its version strings through toCommonEndian, and the game crashed at its main menu.
+template<> inline std::string EndianReverse<std::string>(std::string t) {
+	return t;
+}
+
 inline static bool isBigEndian() {
 	short n = 0x1;
 	return (*(char*)(&n) == 0x0);
